1.0 Introduction
==================
As all TA sysops surely know, Transamiga cannot properly handle dates
past 1999.  This article explains the problems, and how they have been
overcome, ready for Jan 1st 2000.

Sami Radwan has for some 2 years or more been writing a new BBS system
which he proposes to call "TransComm BBS". Unfortunately his university
work has - understandably - taken up so much of his time that it begins
to look as if TransComm BBS may not be published in time for sysops to
become familiar with it before 2000.


2.0 The problems
================

  2.1 (serious)
  -------------
  Transamiga holds the date of a user's last call in line 9 of the user
  record as 6 digits: e.g. 990125.

  When a caller logs in, this date is available to the BBS system with
  the command "Userinfo 'f'", and this returns the last call date in the
  form "25 Jan 99".

  When the caller logs off - when TA obeys "hangup", TA rewrites the
  user record.  If the date is in fact 16 Jan 2000, we would expect the
  last call date to be written as 000116.  But it isn't.  TA discards
  leading zeros, and writes "116" into the lastcall field!  Next time this
  caller logs on and a program asks for date of last call, Transamiga
  tries to interpret this "116" and crashes.

  This problem has been cracked. See below.


  2.2 (trivial)
  --------------
  File dates are written into Files.BBS in each files area as 6 digits.

  This is only a problem if you use the built-in files lister. So the
  remedy is to use an external lister that can add the right century to
  each file date in the list. I have two such listers available:- see below.


  2.3 (very trivial)
  -------------------
  Caller's date of birth is held as 6 digits.

  I'm doing nothing about this unless anyone demands action. This field
  on the user record is documentary only - not used by Transamiga. If
  anyone calls who was born in or after 2000, then by that time I'd expect
  nobody to be using Transamiga anyway!


  2.4 Sundries
  --------------
  Sysops may have custom listers for various purposes, such as searching
  for a file. If they want to doctor a program to include century then
  there's a simple algorithm they can use.

  If the 2-digit year is after '80', make it 1900+. Otherwise make it
  2000+. Just tack the 2 digits onto the front of the year.


  Log file entries: Sami has issued a new TA library which fixes
  these entries (but does not include century digits). The new
  library is included in this 'millenium' package.


3.0 The Solutions
==================
  Before you start altering anything on your BBS system, read this
  section and the next.

  3.1 In principle
  ----------------
  I've written an Arexx utility that reads or writes line 9 of the user
  record. It can be called as:

      rx BBS:utils/Userlast.rexx <r|w> <user name>

  This script is distributed with this "Millenium package".


  3.2 Examples: (see also Logon.trans example below for actual call)
  --------------
  This section shows principles only. In fact the BBS 'node' (line
  number) is appended to the Env: filename to allow for multiple lines.

  a)    rx BBS:utils/Userlast.rexx r Brian Pennywhistle

    This command needs to be early in Logon.trans.

    This will read line 9 of Brian Pennywhistle's record, and write
    the date of last call into ENV:UserLast.

    The format on the user record might be "991225", which would be
    written to ENV:Userlast as "25 Dec 1999". This is the same format
    returned by Transamiga when asked for "Userinfo 'f'".

   *  Programs wanting to know the date of Brian's last call will read
   * it from ENV:Userlast and handle it exactly as they did before after
   * the usual Userinfo 'f'.


  b)    rx BBS:utils/Userlast.rexx w Brian Pennywhistle

    This needs to be in Logoff.trans. Why? Because when a caller logs
    out, Transamiga is told to "hangup" and this starts various
    housekeeping jobs in TA. One of these is to rewrite the user record.

    So after that we can do our own bit of rewriting to line 9. The 'w'
    argument for Userlast.rexx tells it to write the current date into
    the user record, line 9, as 6 digits. e.g. 000116 (for 16 Jan
    2000). NOTE: it must be _six_ digits, as if the century is
    included, TA gets confused and messes with caller's 'time left'.

    If you don't currently use Logoff.trans, then you will have to do so
    if using my methods. It's one of those "automagic" scripts that are
    called if present.


4.0  Specifics
===============
  The new utility "Userlast.rexx" is included with this document.

  4.1 Logon.trans
  -----------------
  a) You must run BBS:utils/Userlast.rexx somewhere early in the script,
     but AFTER you have got 'username' from Transamiga.

        userinfo 'a'; username=result
        systeminfo '8'; bbsline=result
        string='rx BBS:utils/userlast.rexx '||bbsline||':r:'||username
        address command string

     Note: You may be using a different variable for "username". Change
           the above to what you use.
     Notice the user of ':' as argument separator. This is done because
           the user name will usually include a space.

  b) (This only applies if you show or use the date of user's last call.)

    You need to disable "userinfo 'f'" and the result.
    Replace it with the following lines somewhere soon after that line.

        ufile='env:userlast.'||bbsline
        call open('last',ufile,'r')
        lastcall = readln('last')
        call close('last')

    You may need to change the name "lastcall" to whatever you used
    before as the 'result' of Userinfo 'f'.

    The above change will NOT work (and will crash) if you didn't run
    Userlast.rexx as described in part (a) above.


  4.2 Logoff.trans
  -------------------
    You MUST have a Logoff.trans script. Somewhere in this, you need the
    following lines:

        userinfo 'a'   ; username=result
        systeminfo '8'; bbsline=result
        string='rx BBS:utils/userlast.rexx '||bbsline||':w:'||username
        address command string   /* rewrite user file line 9 */

    Note that 'username' is as supplied by Transamiga. It must not be
    'compressed' (space removed).

  4.3  -- IMPORTANT --
  -------------------------
   All TA support programs that need the date of user's last call
   MUST DISABLE  Userinfo 'f' (which will probably crash) and use
   instead the following lines, which will produce exactly the same
   result format but including century. e.g. "21 Feb 2000"

   Adjust the actual variable names to what you are using.

      systeminfo '8'; bbsline=result
      ufile='env:userlast.'||bbsline
      call open('last',ufile,'r')
      lastcall = readln('last')  /* 'lastcall' is the wanted date */
      call close('last')


  4.4  If you have any problem with writing an arexx script, email me
       and I will do my best to help you. I can supply the original
       Transamiga Logon.trans with the new millenium adjustment included.


5.0 Other support programs
===========================
    I have the following Transamiga support utilities which all handle
    the millenium ok. Email me with your requirements.

    Gnew.trans (with binary Gnewfiles) - Included in this package.
         Displays new files since chosen date. Offers user the date of
         his last call as default.

    Filescroller.trans - AVAILABLE ON REQUEST
         Scrolling file area lister with many options including mark files

    GniceEd  - AVAILABLE ON REQUEST
         Easy Graphic Interface for editing of user record.

    QuickList.trans  - AVAILABLE ON REQUEST
         A replacement for the built-in file area lister ('L' command)
         Displays dates with century. Allows file marking.

    Gsearch.trans (with binary Gsearch)   AVAILABLE ON REQUEST
         Allows searching all BBS for a file or description string. Shows
         file dates with century.



6.0 Actions to take when you update for the millenium
===================
The actions described may be taken at any time from now on. They are
fully compatible with ongoing use of Transamiga before 2000.

  Steps 1 to 5 are essential and must all be done as one job. Do one and
  you must do them all.

  1. Make a backup of each file you change. What I do is "clone" the
     file with DirOpus, calling the copy xxxxx.bak

  2. Copy Userlast.rexx into your BBS:utils drawer

  3. Study your own Logon.trans. Disable any line that says "Userinfo 'f'"
      and stores the 'result'.  See para 4.1 above.

      Put into Logon.trans, near the begining but after you've got
      'username' from TA, the coding in para 4.1 above.

     If you did use "Userinfo 'f'", then you must add instead the
     coding that fetches 'lastcall' (or whatever name you use).
     See para 4.1 (b) above for the lines to add.

  4. If you use Logoff.trans, you must add to it the lines in
     Para 4.2 above.

    If you don't currently use Logoff.trans, then you must do so.
    use the example script included with these notes.

  5. Find out which of your support scripts use "Date of last call".
     These will be any program that offers to "List new files".

     Disable "Userinfo 'f'" and the result. Replace this with the
     coding lines in Para 4.3 above.


  6. The rest of the altered programs mentioned above are optional
     extras. Use them or not, as you like. They can be added any time
     later.


